}\b\f0\fs24  Write a brief note on Conjunction and its types. \lang9\b0\fs22\par
\pard\nowidctlpar\lang1033\fs24 Subordinating Conjunctions\par
A subordinating conjunction introduces a dependent clause and indicates the nature of the relationship\par
Among the independent clause(s) and the dependent clause(s).\par
The most common subordinating conjunctions are "after," "although," "as," "because," "before," "how,"\par
"if," "once," "since," "than," "that," "though," "till," "until," "when," "where," "whether," and "while." Each\par
of the highlighted words in the following sentences is a subordinating conjunction:\par
After she had learned to drive, Alice felt more independent.\par
The subordinating conjunction "after" introduces the dependent clause "After she had learned to drive."\par
If the paperwork arrives on time, your cheque will be mailed on Tuesday.\par
Gerald had to begun his thesis over again when his computer cras\par
\pard     \b\par
