}\b\f0\fs24  \b0 Differentiate between countable nouns and concrete nouns. Discuss with examples. \par
\par
Concrete Nouns\par
A concrete noun is a noun which names anything (or anyone) that you can perceive through your physical\par
Senses: touch, sight, taste, hearing, or smell. A concrete noun is the opposite of an abstract noun.\par
The highlighted words in the following sentences are all concrete nouns:\par
The \cf3\b judge\cf0\b0  handed the \cf3\b files\cf0\b0  to the \cf3 clerk\cf0 .\par
Countable Nouns\par
A countable noun (or count noun) is a noun with both a singular and a plural form, and it names anything\par
(or anyone) that you can count. In each of the following sentences, the highlighted words are countable\par
\par
\par
